HYBRID SRAM-Z80 MICROPROCESSOR ARCHITECTURE

A Harvard-Split, Multi-Banked, Self-Caching 8/16-Bit CISC Microprocessor Core with External Commodity SRAM Register & Micro-Snippet Subsystem

1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY & INVENTIVE CONCEPT

The Hybrid SRAM-Z80 re-engineers the classic 1976 Zilog Z80 microarchitecture by physically partitioning its internal storage hierarchy across a shrunk NMOS/CMOS CPU die (~7,500 transistors, ~25% smaller area) and a commodity external 150 ns static RAM subsystem (1024 ? 8-bit, e.g., 2 ? Intel 2114-2) time-multiplexed onto the existing 40-pin DIP bus during idle odd half-T states (T/1).
By retaining cycle-exact 4-T-state M1 timing (4T M1) and keeping latency-critical state (ADDER/ADDES, DRAM_PC, SRAM_PC, WZ, IR, I, and local 4T AF/AF' banks) on-die, the architecture eliminates external DRAM bus contention during inner loops, stack operations, and context switches—achieving near-8086 throughput and 22%–38% higher code density than both the stock Z80 and MOS 6502 while remaining 100% binary-compatible with legacy Z80 software.

2. SILICON FLOORPLAN & ON-DIE MICROARCHITECTURE


2.1 On-Die vs. Off-Die Storage Partitioning

1. On-Die Static/Dynamic Core (~7,500 transistors, ~17 mm? @ 6 µm NMOS):
