1. Architectural Modes & Dual-Opcode (OP OP) Switches

• Cold Reset / Boot State: Starts in strict Z80_8080 binary compatibility mode (STICKY = 0, RR = Main, MODE_V = 0, PACKED_MODE = 0), running 100% of legacy 1974–1976 8080 and Z80 software unmodified.
• CP A + CP A (0xBF 0xBF > Z80_EXTEND): Non-destructively enters Extended ISA mode (RR prefixes in LD r,r, Super-CB, dissolved ED, constant MODE_V baseline). Preserves every general-purpose register (A..SP).
• XOR A + XOR A (0xAF 0xAF > Z80_8080): Exits back to legacy Z80_8080 compatibility mode (and zeroes A).
• LD SP,HL + LD SP,HL (0xF9 0xF9 > Z80_SPECIAL): Persistent until hardware RESET; enables R0–R7 repeaters and the CARRY prefix.
• DI + DI (0xF3 0xF3 > HALT disabled) / EI + EI (0xFB 0xFB > HALT enabled): Replaces legacy 0x76 HALT (freeing 0x76 on the Main Page for 1-byte LD (DE),(V) / LD (DE),(HL)).

2. PACKED Local-SRAM Execution (SRAM_PC[15:0]) & IM 4 Local Interrupts

• Zero Low-ROM Conflict: PACKED_MODE is an explicit hardware state (not address-decoded), leaving external 0x0000–0x03FF 100% free for boot ROMs and legacy interrupt vectors.
• Local 16-Bit SRAM Memory Map (1024B Baseline > 4 KB 1981 On-Die Z800-EX > 64 KB Scalable):
	• 0x0000–0x003F (A[8:0] = 0x000–0x01F): 3 Register Banks (Main, ', '') for BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AW.
	• 0x0040–0x013F: IM 4 Local ISR Vector Table (128 ? 16-bit entries inside local SRAM).
	• 0x0140–0x03FF (scaling up to 0x0FFF on 1981 Z800-EX or 0xFFFF on 64 KB boards): PACKED Local SRAM Code (zero external M1/M2 bus fetches; runs at 100% speed during VDP DMA BUS_USED = 1).
• JP_SRAM n (0xED on Main Page, 2 Bytes): Fetches byte n, freezes external PC at the next instruction (if PACKED_MODE == 0), loads SRAM_PC[15:0] < {6'b0, n[7:0], 2'b00} (n ? 4), and sets PACKED_MODE = 1.
• Native CALL & RET Inside PACKED_MODE: While PACKED_MODE == 1, CALL (V) / CALL rel16 pushes SRAM_PC[15:0] onto (SP) and branches within local SRAM; RET (0xC9) pops SRAM_PC[15:0] from (SP) and remains in PACKED_MODE = 1—scaling seamlessly to 4 KB / 64 KB local SRAM programs.
