PART 2: SUMMARY OF CORE ARCHITECTURAL INVENTIONS

1. 7-Page Orthogonal RR Substitution Engine (LD r,r Self-Loads): Repurposing the 7 dead LD r,r opcodes (40, 49, 52, 5B, 64, 6D, 7F) into 7 uniform RR prefix pages (BC, DE, HL, SP, AW, DD, FD) dissolved the entire Z80 ED prefix page (~22 PLA terms saved), added 16-bit relative branching (JP rel16 / CALL rel16), and removed all collision-guard logic—resulting in a smaller PLA (~78 terms vs. 109) than the 1976 Z80.
2. Asymmetric 3-Bank Shadow Bridging (Main, ', ''): Decoupling Left (dst) and Right (src) register-bank routing via DD/FD (' Shadow 1) and BC/DE/AW ('' Shadow 2) enables single-instruction cross-context data transfer without EXX swapping or temporary buffers.
3. Immediate-Indexed Super-CB Page (0xCB): Moving the bit index n[2:0] to an immediate byte and discarding sideband register loads collapsed 192 redundant bit opcodes + DDCB/FDCB into one unified Super-CB page supporting all 11 bit/shift ops across all 8 base registers, all 6 (RR) pointers, all 6 (RR+d) displaced pointers, and (nn) absolute addresses.
4. Constructive Block Primitives + R0–R7 Replay Engine: Replacing rigid LDI..OTDR microcode with composable INCINC, DECDEC, REPBC, REPNZ, REPB, memory-to-memory transfers (LD (DE),(V), LD (BC),(RR+d), PUSH/POP (RR+d), EX (DE),(RR+d)), ACT_CC predication, and R0–R7 (0..63?) instruction-latch replay eliminates M1 fetches inside tight loops.
5. Stripped-Die + 16-Bit Local SRAM (~6,540 Transistors) & Always-Gate VDP Concurrency: Offloading BC, DE, HL, IX, IY, AW to external 16-bit SRAM (leaving only 10 bytes of static latches A, F, SP, PC, WZ, SRAM_PC on-die) cuts die transistor count by ~23%. Using /SRAM_CS = /M1 OR /RFSH with /FILTERED_M1, /FILTERED_RFSH, a 1k?–2.2k? resistive isolation matrix, Always-Gate 74LS245 transceivers, and /BUSACK (BUS_USED) feedback allows the CPU to run register ops, PACKED SRAM routines (JP_SRAM/RET_SRAM), and IM 4 local interrupts at 100% speed simultaneously during VDP DMA—and scales seamlessly to the 1981–1982 Z800-EX (1–4 KB on-die SRAM at 8–12 MHz) and 1997 high-pin-count / 40+ MHz implementations.

